{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>Torn, torn.</b> It intends to say that there were two acts of tearing: the first, that a wild beast attacked him and killed him; and the second, that it dragged his body to its den — for by this Yaakov despaired of searching for his bones to bury them. And without this, it would be difficult: why did Yaakov not exert himself to search for Yosef's bones in order to fulfill with them the commandment of burial? And the brothers were cunning and said, ״זֹאת מָצָאנוּ״ — meaning, this tunic alone, without bones with it. From this he perceived that the wild beast had dragged it to its den, and therefore he did not trouble them to search further for his bones:"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:46:50.376779120Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}